\begin{abstract}
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into enterprise platforms, requiring business users to write prompts despite the lack of prompt engineering expertise. Although these users possess domain knowledge, they struggle to translate their intentions into effective prompts and often overlook established practices like few-shot learning. We present Prompt Editor, a human-in-the-loop system that assists users in writing high-quality prompts by initializing and improving user-created prompts. \ewu{by leveraging an organization's existing corpus of prompts to both initialize and improve user-created prompts}. At its core, Prompt Editor leverages a taxonomy of prompt segment types learned from an organization's existing collection of prompts. This taxonomy captures the contextual relationships between different components of effective prompts, similar to how Abstract Syntax Trees represent programming language structure. \ewu{This supports alignment of prompt fragments and recommendation} We demonstrate Prompt Editor with a hypothetical LLM-based enterprise platform that extracts structured data from customer emails. Attendees can interact with the demo to experience first-hand how Prompt Editor supports prompt writing.

% Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into enterprise platforms, requiring business users to write prompts despite lack of relevant expertise. Although these users possess domain knowledge, they struggle to translate their intentions into effective prompts and often overlook established prompt engineering techniques like few-shot learning. We present Prompt Editor, a human-in-the-loop system that enables users to write high-quality prompts. At its core, Prompt Editor leverages a learned taxonomy of prompt segment types that captures the contextual relationships between different components of effective prompts, similar to how Abstract Syntax Trees represent programming language structure. We introduce an iterative method to learn this taxonomy from existing prompts and demonstrate how it enables automated analysis of prompt completeness and quality. The system provides targeted feedback by identifying missing essential components and evaluating each prompt segment against predefined rules. Our demonstration showcases how Prompt Editor can be integrated into existing text editors, making prompt engineering more accessible to business users while enforcing best practices. Through this approach, we bridge the gap between domain expertise and prompt engineering capabilities in enterprise settings.

