\section{Introduction}
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into enterprise business platforms to extract insights from unstructured data and perform complex reasoning tasks. These platforms rely on input prompts to guide the LLM's behavior, encoding important task-specific information. To empower users to take advantage of the full potential of LLM, many enterprise companies have turned business users into prompt writers. Examples include Celonis' Annotation Builder platform \cite{CelonisAB}, Palantir’s Artificial Intelligence platform \cite{PalantirAIP} and Salesforce’s AgentForce platform \cite{SalesforceAgentforce}.

However, while business users can bring their domain expertise, they lack the skills to craft effective prompts. Studies have identified significant barriers that prevent users from translating their intent into effective prompts \cite{SixLearningBarriers, WhyJohnnyCantPrompt}, and many users struggle to create even the first version of a prompt. Furthermore, business users are often unaware of prompt engineering techniques, such as few-shot examples and chain-of-thought prompting, both of which have been shown to improve performance \cite{brown2020languagemodelsfewshotlearners, wei2023chainofthoughtpromptingelicitsreasoning}.

Existing best practices for prompt engineering suggest organizing prompt information into discrete sections or "prompt segments", much like a structured document \cite{OpenAIPromptEngineering}. 
