\textbf{Taxonomy of Prompt Segment Types.} Our key insight is that prompts behave similarly to programming languages \cite{wang2024langgptrethinkingstructuredreusable, PromptEngineeringHottestProgrammingLanguage, AIWarMasteringArtOfPromptEngineering} where the functional meaning of a sentence depends on how it is situated among surrounding elements. For example, a sentence such as 'Write me an email about the acceptance of a sales order' could function as a "\textit{Task Instruction}" if it directly conveys the user’s goal, or it could be an "\textit{Example}" if it illustrates a possible user input in an example. A naive flat schema cannot capture these context-sensitive roles because it cannot account for how one part of the prompt influences another. Building on the programming language analogy, we propose modeling prompts with a taxonomy of prompt segment types, as seen in Figure \ref{fig:taxonomy}, similar to an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Each node in this taxonomy represents a distinct function (e.g., specifying the LLM’s role, giving examples) and is connected through is-part-of relationships that encode the hierarchical structure of the prompt. In this way, the taxonomy captures not only what a given segment contains, but also how it fits within the broader prompt context.
