\textbf{Learning a Taxonomy from prompts.} Recent work \cite{wan2024tntllmtextminingscale} has demonstrated that LLMs are capable of generating taxonomies of labels from document summaries. Building on these findings, we use LLMs to infer the function or utility of each prompt segment, thus addressing the shortcoming of purely semantic topic modeling approaches. By iteratively segmenting prompts and examining leaf segments, we identify more granular child nodes that serve distinct functional roles. The is-part-of relationship ensures that a parent segment can be refined into subsegments, mirroring how a programming construct can be broken into smaller functional blocks. Once the iterative segmentation process is complete, an expert reviews and consolidates the resulting taxonomy, ensuring it remains both practical and faithful to the functional structure of real-world prompts.

