\paragraph{Prompt Improvement}
For improving prompts, Prompt Editor acts like a compiler for prompts, parsing the user's input prompt into taxonomy segments and evaluating the prompt on best practices. This involves making sure that (1) all essential segments are present and (2) each segment adheres to predefined rules. For example, a rule for the 'Example' segment type might require that each example include a reasoning behind the model’s output. This ensures that the model is not only exposed to input/output pairs but also benefits from reasoning to improve its understanding. Lastly, we surface the feedback to the user and provide them with the autonomy to evaluate suggestions and potentially tweak changes according to their domain knowledge and understanding of the task. In this way, users effectively supervise the Prompt Editor in the prompt improvement process, whereas the Prompt Editor acts as a quality gate and generates ideas for users to edit the prompt to reach those quality standards.

