\section{System Description}

\subsection{System Overview}

\Cref{fig:arch} describes the overall system to support the prompt editor.  It consists of two main components, the prompt analyzer that uses the currently learned taxonomy to support the editor and the taxonomy learner that analyzes the corpus of prompts to refine the current taxonomy.  

The user interacts with the editor by first describing a high-level task, upon which the prompt analyzer identifies a subset of the taxonomy that is relevant for the task.   It then initializes the prompt by selecting example text from the corpus for each segment and using an LLM to generate an initial prompt based on the task and examples.   The user can then \ewu{describe high level actions the user can take, and what the analyzer does.  Some text in S2.3 can probably go here}.

The prompt analyzer relies on a high quality prompt taxonomy that the taxonomy learner builds and maintains.   It takes as input the corpus of prompts, past task descriptions, and user feedback to segment prompts, match each segment to nodes in the taxonomy, and augment the taxonomy with new nodes if needed.   We describe this problem formulation in \Cref{ss:learner}.   This component also leverages downstream quality signals (e.g., from deployed prompts and user feedback) as well as the high-level task descriptions to evaluate the efficacy of the prompt segmentations, match tasks with taxonomy segment, and identify high-quality example text for each segment type.   
The following subsections describe the taxonomy learning problem.

%\ewu{Jeff: Done; Describe overall system -- both learning and prompt editor main components} Our system addresses the challenge of capturing prompt engineering standards and task-specific prompt best practices by learning a taxonomy from a collection of past prompts. Using this learned taxonomy, we provide the Prompt Editor as a prompt development tool to reduce the effort users need to create prompts that align with such best practices.

