\subsubsection{Prompt Improvement}

Given an input prompt \( \mathcal{P} \), a predefined taxonomy \( \mathcal{T} \), and a set of predefined rules or guidelines associated with each segment type, Prompt Editor returns feedback of two types:
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{images/guideline-based-feedback.png}
    \caption{Guideline-based feedback}
    \label{fig:Guideline-based-feedback}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{Guideline-based feedback:} Feedback specific to segments in the prompt that fail one of the defined quality guidelines. These are returned as highlighted suggestions within the prompt text, offering actionable advice linked to specific text positions.
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{images/missing-segment-feedback.png}
    \caption{Missing-segment feedback}
    \label{fig:Missing-segment-feedback}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{Missing-Segment feedback:} Suggestions for missing segment types, independent of specific text positions.
    % \item \textbf{Missing-Segment feedback:} Suggestions for missing segment types, independent of specific text positions.
\\
The system consists of the following components, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:system-flow}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Prompt Segmentation:} The segmentation component takes the taxonomy \( \mathcal{T} \) and the prompt text \( \mathcal{P} \) as input and performs segmentation through an LLM call to return a list of prompt segments for each segment type defined in the taxonomy. First, the LLM is instructed to parse $\mathcal{P}$ into individual sentences. Since there could be overlaps in segments, each sentence is then classified into a segment type defined by $\mathcal{T}$ through multiclass classification. A post-processing script groups consecutive sentences with the same classification into cohesive segments, forming a structured representation of the prompt. This structured representation serves as the foundation for feedback generation.
    \item \textit{Missing-Segment Feedback:} After segmentation, the system evaluates which segment types in \( \mathcal{T} \) are missing. For each missing segment, the LLM generates a brief rationale explaining how its inclusion could enhance task performance. The system also provides a suggested text for the missing segment, giving users a starting point to complete their prompt.
    \item \textit{Guideline-Based Feedback:} The system uses a separate LLM call to evaluate each prompt segment against predefined rules for its corresponding taxonomy element. For example, the guideline for an 'Instruction' segment might require the inclusion of explicit task constraints. Segments that do not meet these guidelines are flagged, and the LLM generates feedback that aggregates the specific rule violations into a cohesive suggestion.
    \item \textit{Locating Segments in the Prompt:} Feedback generated by the Guideline-Based Feedback component is associated with its corresponding prompt segment. The system performs string matching and identifies the text position of each segment within \( \mathcal{P} \), allowing the feedback to surface inline within the editor. This ensures that users receive contextually relevant suggestions directly within the text.
\end{enumerate}
The missing-segment feedback and guideline-based feedback with text positions are then returned to the frontend and rendered through the text editor's UI components.

\section{Demonstration Scenario}
\ewu{Jeff: I included the screenshots in the Systems section; good to have a mockup of a screenshot or screenshots of what the user may actually see and use in the demo!  Easier to talk about features and so on.}
Our demonstration allows attendees to get a concrete understanding of how Prompt Editor supports users to initialize a prompt from scratch (Prompt Initialization) and iteratively refine prompts (Prompt Improvement). 

\textbf{Example Use Case.} The prompts are crafted for a hypothetical LLM-based enterprise platform that allows users to create LLM prompts that extract structured data from unstructured text within customer data, such as a database of emails, based on the provided prompt.

To illustrate both the prompt initialization and prompt improvement functionalities, we use a scenario where a business user develops an LLM prompt on the platform to analyze email text. The LLM’s task is to determine whether an email pertains to a sales order confirmation and, if so, extract the sales order ID.

\textbf{Scripted Demonstration} For prompt initialization, we demonstrate it by providing "Determine if an email is for a sales order confirmation and extract the sales order ID" as the short task description. The system then generates an initialized prompt that is filled in the empty text editor.
For prompt improvement, we will provide an example of a poorly-written prompt, then show how the system identifies missing segments and provides guideline-based feedback for improving the prompt.

\textbf{Interactive Demonstration} Attendees will also have the opportunity to write their own short task descriptions for prompt initialization or their own prompts for prompt improvement in the same use case. In doing so, they can experience firsthand how the Prompt Editor reduces efforts in prompt writing and enforces best practices.

